<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagaruyung</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Istano Basa Pagaruyung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pagaruyung is a village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanah-datar">Tanah Datar</a> regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, the seat of the Minangkabau royal house from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a> in the 14th century. The royal family was largely killed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padri-war">Padri War</a> around 1815. A large replica of the royal palace, the Istano Basa, stands there today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagaruyung.jpg" alt="Pagaruyung" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RahmatdenasMuffin Wizard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Minang.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pagaruyung lies in Tanjung Emas subdistrict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanah-datar">Tanah Datar</a> regency, near the town of Batusangkar. Its name derives from a Minangkabau name for the nibung or ruyung tree, and the kingdom it named was also called Malayapura; the seal of Sultan Tunggul Alam Bagagar calls it Pagaruyung Darul Qarar. The highlands around it were long reputed rich in gold. In 1684 the Portuguese explorer Thomas Dias, sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, reported, probably from hearsay, a palace entered through three gates and a people who lived by gold panning and agriculture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An inscription carved on the back of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghapasa">Amoghapasa</a> statue records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a> proclaimed himself king in Malayapura in 1347. He is believed to have ruled central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> until about 1375, at first as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and most likely to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>. Inscribed stones and statues of his reign were found at Bukit Gombak, a hill near the village where a royal palace is believed to have stood. By the 16th century royal power was shared by three kings, of the world, of custom and of religion, known together as the Rajo Tigo Selo.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread through teachers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, and in the 17th century the kingdom became a sultanate. Early in that century it was forced to recognise the Sultanate of Aceh, but the west coast rose against Aceh's governors around 1665, and the Dutch Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie (VOC), which had opened a trading office at Padang, drove Aceh from the coast in 1666. Pagaruyung's rulers then dealt with both the Dutch and the British, and congratulated the British when they took Padang during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a> in 1781.</p><p>The palace on Batu Patah Hill was burned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padri-war">Padri War</a> in 1804. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a> came in 1818 he found that the capital had been burned three times and abandoned, leaving little more than waringin trees. On 10 February 1821 Sultan Tunggul Alam Bagagarsyah and 19 other traditional leaders signed an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> against the Padri, which the Dutch took as the kingdom's submission. The last king, Sultan Arifin Muningsyah, returned in 1824, died in 1825 and was buried at Pagaruyung. Bagagarsyah, made only Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanah-datar">Tanah Datar</a>, was arrested for treason on 2 May 1833 and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pagaruyung kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1773 the chiefs invited Raja Melewar, a prince of the <b>Pagaruyung</b> royal house, to become their overlord, and he was installed as the first Yang di-Pertuan Besar at Seri Menanti.</blockquote><a href="/state/negeri-sembilan">Negeri Sembilan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1347 Adityawarman, a prince who had served Majapahit, proclaimed himself king of Malayu at Dharmasraya and moved his court up into the Minangkabau highlands, near <b>Pagaruyung</b> in the Tanah Datar valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagaruyung-kingdom">Pagaruyung Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
